{"name":"Dead Pixel","hex":"#3b3a3a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dead pixel","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A pixel on an LCD that does not light up.","a miscolored pixel on an image where it is otherwise not intended to be."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dead_pixel"}],"entries":[{"title":"Defective pixel","blocks":[{"text":"A defective pixel or a dead pixel is a pixel on a liquid crystal display (LCD) that is not functioning properly. The ISO standard ISO 13406-2 distinguishes between three different types of defective pixels, while hardware companies tend to have further distinguishing types."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/20/Lcd_display_dead_pixel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Defective_pixel"},{"title":"Death","blocks":[{"text":"Death is the end of an organism's life. It is the irreversible cessation of biological functions that sustain a living organism. Entropy, nature's tendency toward disorder, can contribute to the breakdown of living systems over time. Upon death, organisms stop exhibiting behavior and generally begin to decompose through various physical and chemical processes. Death can occur at any stage of development, including in infancy. A person may experience clinical death that is reversible, resulting in some cases in a near-death experience. In humans, establishing death of the brain determines that an individual has irreversibly died."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Hendrick_Andriessen_-_Vanity_Piece_-_1914-DE_-_Museum_of_Fine_Arts_Ghent_%28MSK%29.jpg/330px-Hendrick_Andriessen_-_Vanity_Piece_-_1914-DE_-_Museum_of_Fine_Arts_Ghent_%28MSK%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death"},{"title":"Pixel","blocks":[{"text":"In digital imaging, a pixel, pel, or picture element is the smallest addressable element of a raster image. In a display device, dot matrix printer, or ASCII art a pixel is the smallest element whose output can generally be controlled independently."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Pixel-example.png/330px-Pixel-example.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pixel"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Damaged_photo_sensitive_chip.jpg/500px-Damaged_photo_sensitive_chip.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Damaged_photo_sensitive_chip.jpg/500px-Damaged_photo_sensitive_chip.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A photograph taken with a damaged image sensor","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADamaged_photo_sensitive_chip.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Deadpixelsinphonescreen1.jpg/500px-Deadpixelsinphonescreen1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Deadpixelsinphonescreen1.jpg/500px-Deadpixelsinphonescreen1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Dark dot defect in a phone screen","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADeadpixelsinphonescreen1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Dead-pixels.jpg/250px-Dead-pixels.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/56/Dead-pixels.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/Dead-pixels.jpg/250px-Dead-pixels.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=